"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkcasdoor_website=globalThis.webpackChunkcasdoor_website||[]).push([[9004],{93846(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","description":"Casdoor has an official Helm chart. It lives in the main repository under manifests/casdoor, is published with every Casdoor release to GHCR, and is listed on Artifact Hub. Starting with Casdoor v4.18.0, the chart can also hold your organizations, applications, users and providers as declarative configuration: change them in the values file, run helm upgrade, and the running pods apply the change without a restart.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/kubernetes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Helm","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/helm"},{"inline":true,"label":"GitOps","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/git-ops"},{"inline":true,"label":"Configuration as Code","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/configuration-as-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":5.45,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","Configuration as Code","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"}},"content":"Casdoor has an official Helm chart. It lives in the main repository under [`manifests/casdoor`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor), is published with every Casdoor release to GHCR, and is listed on [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor). Starting with Casdoor v4.18.0, the chart can also hold your organizations, applications, users and providers as declarative configuration: change them in the values file, run `helm upgrade`, and the running pods apply the change without a restart.\\n\\nThis post walks through a Kubernetes deployment from the first `helm install` to a setup you can keep in Git.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Install\\n\\nYou need Helm v3.8+ and a cluster:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nThe chart version is the Casdoor version, so `--version 4.18.0` installs Casdoor 4.18.0. Without an Ingress or Gateway, the service is only reachable inside the cluster. To try it, forward a port:\\n\\n```shell\\nkubectl port-forward svc/casdoor 8000:8000\\n```\\n\\nThen open `http://localhost:8000` and sign in with `admin` / `123`.\\n\\n## A production setup\\n\\nThe defaults are meant for a quick try: Casdoor stores its data in SQLite inside the container, so it is lost when the pod is replaced. For a real deployment, put the settings below in a `values.yaml` file and install with `helm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor -f values.yaml`.\\n\\n### External database\\n\\n```yaml\\ndatabase:\\n  driver: postgres # or mysql, cockroachdb\\n  host: postgres.db.svc.cluster.local\\n  user: casdoor\\n  password: change-me\\n  databaseName: casdoor\\n  sslMode: require\\n```\\n\\nTo keep the database password out of the values file, put the whole `app.conf` in a Secret and set `configFromSecret` to its name.\\n\\n### Exposing Casdoor\\n\\nThe chart supports both a classic Ingress and the Gateway API. With an Ingress controller and cert-manager:\\n\\n```yaml\\ningress:\\n  enabled: true\\n  className: nginx\\n  annotations:\\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\\n  hosts:\\n    - host: door.example.com\\n      paths:\\n        - path: /\\n          pathType: Prefix\\n  tls:\\n    - secretName: casdoor-tls\\n      hosts:\\n        - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nWith the Gateway API (Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Kong, NGINX Gateway Fabric and others), attach an `HTTPRoute` to an existing Gateway:\\n\\n```yaml\\ngatewayApi:\\n  enabled: true\\n  parentRefs:\\n    - name: my-gateway\\n      namespace: gateway-system\\n  hostnames:\\n    - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nThe chart can also create the Gateway itself and add an HTTP to HTTPS redirect, see the [Helm guide](/docs/basic/try-with-helm#exposing-casdoor).\\n\\n### More than one replica\\n\\nCasdoor keeps sign-in sessions on local disk by default. Before running more than one replica, point all of them to the same Redis, so a user stays signed in whichever pod serves the request. Every `app.conf` setting can be passed as an environment variable, so the endpoint can come from a Secret:\\n\\n```yaml\\nreplicaCount: 3\\n\\nenvFromSecret:\\n  - name: redisEndpoint\\n    secretName: casdoor-redis\\n    key: endpoint # host:port, or host:port,db,password\\n```\\n\\nThe same Redis is used for the OAuth device flow state. Once Redis is in place, `autoscaling.enabled: true` adds a HorizontalPodAutoscaler.\\n\\n## Configuration as code\\n\\nClicking organizations and applications together in the web UI is fine for a first test, but most teams want the setup of their identity provider reviewed and versioned like the rest of their infrastructure. The `initData` values do that:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  data:\\n    organizations:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: acme\\n        displayName: Acme\\n        passwordType: bcrypt\\n    applications:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: app-acme-portal\\n        organization: acme\\n        displayName: Acme Portal\\n        redirectUris:\\n          - https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\n    users:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: alice\\n        displayName: Alice\\n        password: change-me\\n        signupApplication: app-acme-portal\\n    roles:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: admins\\n        displayName: Admins\\n        users: [acme/alice]\\n        isEnabled: true\\n```\\n\\nThe chart stores the data in a Secret and mounts it into the pods. Casdoor applies it at startup, then checks it every 30 seconds (`initData.watchInterval`). When you change the data and run `helm upgrade`, kubelet updates the mounted Secret and the running pods apply it without a restart.\\n\\nA few rules make this safe to use alongside the web UI:\\n\\n- **Only the fields you write are managed.** An object that already exists gets the fields set in the file; its other fields keep the values edited in the UI. You can manage just the redirect URIs of an application, and leave the rest to the admin console.\\n- **Passwords are initial passwords.** A user\'s `password` is only used when the user is created, so users can change theirs afterwards.\\n- **Nothing is deleted.** Removing an object from the file doesn\'t remove it from Casdoor.\\n- **Errors don\'t take Casdoor down.** If an object fails to apply, the error is logged and Casdoor keeps serving; the data is retried on every check until it applies.\\n\\nThe file format is the one Casdoor has always used for [data initialization](/docs/deployment/data-initialization): organizations, applications, users, providers, certs, roles, permissions, groups, LDAP servers, webhooks, plans, pricings and more, with the same field names as the REST API. A quick way to get started is to export an existing instance with `casdoor -export` and keep the objects you want to manage.\\n\\nTo keep the data out of the values file, for example when it is managed by Sealed Secrets or External Secrets, create the Secret yourself:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  existingSecret: casdoor-init-data\\n  existingSecretKey: init_data.yaml\\n```\\n\\nThe same feature works without Kubernetes. Mount a JSON or YAML file and set:\\n\\n```ini\\ninitDataFile = /init-data/init_data.yaml\\ninitDataMerge = true\\ninitDataWatchInterval = 30\\n```\\n\\n## Or use Terraform\\n\\nIf you prefer `terraform plan`, imports of existing objects and drift detection, the official [Terraform provider](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/casdoor/casdoor) manages the same objects through the Casdoor API:\\n\\n```terraform\\nresource \\"casdoor_application\\" \\"portal\\" {\\n  name          = \\"app-acme-portal\\"\\n  organization  = \\"acme\\"\\n  redirect_uris = [\\"https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nUse the Helm values when the configuration should ship with the deployment, and Terraform when Casdoor is one of many systems you already manage with it. See the [Terraform guide](/docs/deployment/terraform).\\n\\n## Coming from the old chart\\n\\nUp to v4.15.0, the chart was published as `oci://registry-1.docker.io/casbin/casdoor-helm-charts`, and the separate `casdoor/casdoor-helm` repository is now archived. The Docker Hub chart still receives every release, but new installs should use the GHCR one. Moving an existing release over is a regular upgrade, and resource names stay the same:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm upgrade casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor --version <version>\\n```\\n\\n## Links\\n\\n- Helm chart: [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [source](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor)\\n- Guides: [Try with Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm) \xb7 [Data initialization and configuration as code](/docs/deployment/data-initialization#configuration-as-code) \xb7 [Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform)\\n- Don\'t want to run it yourself? [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing?utm_source=casdoor.ai&utm_medium=blog&utm_content=kubernetes) gives you a dedicated Casdoor instance that we host and keep upgraded for you."},{"id":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","description":"On February 22, 2026, Casdoor joined the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape, under the Security & Compliance category in Provisioning. The Landscape brings together widely used cloud-native projects and products; inclusion helps users and enterprises discover and evaluate solutions for building cloud-native systems.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CNCF","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/cncf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud Native","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/cloud-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"Landscape","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/landscape"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":1.95,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CNCF","Cloud Native","Landscape","IAM","SSO","Security"],"date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Running Casdoor on Kubernetes: Official Helm Chart and Configuration as Code","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Am\xe9lioration de la s\xe9curit\xe9 des applications et de l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur avec l\'authentification faciale SSO de Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"On February 22, 2026, **Casdoor** joined the [CNCF Cloud Native Landscape](https://landscape.cncf.io/), under the **Security & Compliance** category in Provisioning. The Landscape brings together widely used cloud-native projects and products; inclusion helps users and enterprises discover and evaluate solutions for building cloud-native systems.\\n\\n[https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<img src=\\"/img/blog/cncf-landscape-casdoor.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor on CNCF Landscape - Security and Compliance section\\" style={{maxWidth: \'100%\', height: \'auto\', border: \'1px solid #eee\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n\\n_Casdoor on the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape (Security & Compliance)_\\n\\n## \xc0 propos de Casdoor\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/casdoor-logo_1185x256.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nCasdoor is an open-source, UI-first Identity and Access Management (IAM) / Single Sign-On (SSO) platform with a web UI. It supports OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML, and CAS, as well as many social logins (Google, GitHub, WeChat, etc.), and helps you build user management and SSO in a few steps. Casdoor is developed by [Casbin](https://casbin.org) in Golang and React. It uses a front-end/back-end separation architecture, supports high concurrency, and is actively maintained by the community. The project has received over 13k GitHub stars.\\n\\n## About CNCF and the Cloud Native Landscape\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1.5rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://www.cncf.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/cncf-main-site-logo.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n<img src=\\"https://landscape.cncf.io/images/logo_header.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF Cloud Native Landscape\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nThe **Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)** is a Linux Foundation project that promotes cloud-native technologies. Its vision is to make cloud native ubiquitous. CNCF maintains the **Cloud Native Landscape** ([https://landscape.cncf.io](https://landscape.cncf.io)), which classifies and tracks projects and products in the cloud-native ecosystem and serves as a reference map for developers and enterprises.\\n\\n## Looking ahead\\n\\nJoining the CNCF Landscape reflects progress in Casdoor\u2019s maturity and community. We will keep improving Casdoor and its integration with cloud-native workflows, and we welcome more users and contributors to join the community.\\n\\nLiens connexes :\\n\\n- [Casdoor](https://casdoor.org) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [Documentation](https://casdoor.org/docs) \xb7 [CNCF Landscape \u2013 Casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?selected=casdoor)"},{"id":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","title":"Am\xe9lioration de la s\xe9curit\xe9 des applications et de l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur avec l\'authentification faciale SSO de Casdoor","description":"\xc0 une \xe9poque o\xf9 la gestion de l\'identit\xe9 num\xe9rique est cruciale, les solutions de Single Sign-On (SSO) comme Casdoor r\xe9volutionnent la mani\xe8re dont les utilisateurs interagissent avec plusieurs applications. Le SSO de Casdoor simplifie le processus de connexion en permettant aux utilisateurs de s\'authentifier une seule fois pour acc\xe9der \xe0 plusieurs services. Cela am\xe9liore non seulement la commodit\xe9 pour l\'utilisateur, mais augmente \xe9galement l\'engagement et la satisfaction.","date":"2023-12-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Single-Sign-On","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/single-sign-on"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face Recogniztion","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/face-recogniztion"},{"inline":true,"label":"FaceID","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/face-id"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/face"},{"inline":true,"label":"Machine Learning","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/machine-learning"}],"readingTime":2.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"James Lawson","title":"Chef de produit","url":"https://casdoor.org","imageURL":"https://cdn.casbin.com/casdoor/static/admin.png","key":"James Lawson","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","title":"Am\xe9lioration de la s\xe9curit\xe9 des applications et de l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur avec l\'authentification faciale SSO de Casdoor","authors":["James Lawson"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Single-Sign-On","Face Recogniztion","FaceID","Face","Machine Learning"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor Joins CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Bienvenue","permalink":"/fr/blog/welcome"}},"content":"\xc0 une \xe9poque o\xf9 la gestion de l\'identit\xe9 num\xe9rique est cruciale, les solutions de Single Sign-On (SSO) comme Casdoor r\xe9volutionnent la mani\xe8re dont les utilisateurs interagissent avec plusieurs applications. Le SSO de Casdoor simplifie le processus de connexion en permettant aux utilisateurs de s\'authentifier une seule fois pour acc\xe9der \xe0 plusieurs services. Cela am\xe9liore non seulement la commodit\xe9 pour l\'utilisateur, mais augmente \xe9galement l\'engagement et la satisfaction.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nPour les entreprises, le SSO de Casdoor rationalise la gestion des identifiants des utilisateurs, am\xe9liorant la s\xe9curit\xe9 et simplifiant l\'int\xe9gration de nouveaux utilisateurs. Il contre le risque associ\xe9 aux mots de passe faibles ou recycl\xe9s en centralisant la gestion des utilisateurs.\\n\\nCependant, face \xe0 la croissance des menaces en ligne, int\xe9grer des mesures de s\xe9curit\xe9 robustes dans les syst\xe8mes SSO comme Casdoor est vital. C\'est l\xe0 que la technologie d\'authentification faciale entre en jeu, ajoutant une couche suppl\xe9mentaire de protection tout en maintenant des interactions conviviales avec l\'utilisateur.\\n\\nL\'authentification faciale utilise des caract\xe9ristiques faciales uniques pour confirmer les identit\xe9s des utilisateurs. Elle compare les images en direct avec des mod\xe8les faciaux stock\xe9s, offrant un moyen s\xe9curis\xe9 mais simple d\'authentifier les utilisateurs. Cette m\xe9thode pr\xe9sente une grande pr\xe9cision, m\xeame dans des conditions vari\xe9es, et est largement adopt\xe9e dans les appareils grand public modernes.\\n\\nL\'int\xe9gration de l\'authentification faciale dans le syst\xe8me SSO de Casdoor promet une exp\xe9rience utilisateur s\xe9curis\xe9e, efficace et fluide. Pour garantir des performances optimales, il est crucial de se concentrer sur :\\n\\n1. _Mesures anti-spoofing_ : La mise en \u0153uvre de la d\xe9tection de vivacit\xe9, comme le suivi des mouvements des yeux ou des gestes al\xe9atoires, est essentielle pour contrer les tentatives de spoofing et maintenir l\'int\xe9grit\xe9 du syst\xe8me.\\n\\n2. _Mises \xe0 jour r\xe9guli\xe8res pour une pr\xe9cision am\xe9lior\xe9e_ : Garder la technologie d\'authentification faciale \xe0 jour avec les derni\xe8res avanc\xe9es en IA et en biom\xe9trie assure la pr\xe9cision et am\xe9liore l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur. Il est vital d\'utiliser des ensembles de donn\xe9es diversifi\xe9s pour la formation des algorithmes afin de pr\xe9venir les biais.\\n\\n3. _\xc9quilibrer la s\xe9curit\xe9 et la commodit\xe9_ : Les meilleurs syst\xe8mes trouvent un \xe9quilibre parfait entre des mesures de s\xe9curit\xe9 strictes et la commodit\xe9 pour l\'utilisateur. Cette approche encourage l\'adoption par les utilisateurs en garantissant un processus d\'authentification simple sans compromettre la s\xe9curit\xe9.\\n\\nEn conclusion, en int\xe9grant l\'authentification faciale avec le SSO de Casdoor, les entreprises peuvent offrir une exp\xe9rience de connexion s\xe9curis\xe9e, efficace et conviviale. Cela non seulement renforce la s\xe9curit\xe9, mais s\'aligne \xe9galement avec les attentes des utilisateurs modernes dans le monde num\xe9rique."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","title":"Bienvenue","description":"Bienvenue sur le site officiel de Casdoor !\ud83d\udc4b","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Authentication","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth2.0","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"Casbin","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casbin"}],"readingTime":1.49,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"ErikQQY","title":"Membre Casbin","url":"https://github.com/ErikQQY","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52615090?s=120&v=4","key":"ErikQQY","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Bienvenue","authors":["ErikQQY"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Authentication","OAuth2.0","Casbin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Am\xe9lioration de la s\xe9curit\xe9 des applications et de l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur avec l\'authentification faciale SSO de Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Bienvenue sur le site officiel de Casdoor !\ud83d\udc4b\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Qu\'est-ce que Casdoor ?\\n\\nCasdoor is a UI-first [Identity Access Management (IAM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_management) / [Single-Sign-On (SSO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_sign-on) platform based on OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML and CAS.\\n\\nCasdoor is developed by [Casbin](https://casbin.org) using Golang and React.\\n\\nCasdoor vise \xe0 fournir une plateforme d\'authentification puissante pour g\xe9rer la gestion des utilisateurs.\\n\\n## Pourquoi utiliser Casdoor ?\\n\\n- \u26a1\ufe0f Fonctionnalit\xe9 puissante et configuration simple. With support for  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_google.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_github.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_facebook.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_twitter.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_linkedin.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_weibo.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wechat.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_qq.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_dingtalk.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>   <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitee.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wecom.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img><img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitlab.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> sign in and traditional username/password sign in, Casdoor helps you implement your users management system just in a few steps.\\n\\n- \ud83d\udca5 Architecture de s\xe9paration front-end back-end. Casdoor fournit une interface utilisateur web man\u0153uvrable et supporte une haute concurrence.\\n\\n- \ud83e\udd42 Communaut\xe9 open source active. Casdoor est open source et activement maintenu.\\n\\n- \ud83c\udf10 Support multilingue. Casdoor prend en charge plusieurs langues, y compris le chinois, l\'anglais, le fran\xe7ais et bien d\'autres.\\n\\n## Comment utiliser Casdoor ?\\n\\nYou can follow the detailed document to learn about [Casdoor API](https://door.casdoor.com/swagger/) and [setup your own Casdoor](/docs/basic/server-installation). Or use the [Casdoor SDKs](/docs/how-to-connect/sdk) to implement your own application."}]}}')}}]);